-/// …AND THE FOLD, THREADED: fight the runs in parallel and add each one AS IT
-/// ARRIVES, in RUN ORDER.  Floating-point addition is not associative, so the
-/// order the marginals are added in is part of the answer — and it is the one
-/// `shard_onto` uses: run 0, then run 1, … then run `count-1`.  One lane is
-/// `shard_onto` itself.
-///
-/// WHAT CHANGED IS WHEN THE SHARDS EXIST.  This used to fight all `count` runs
-/// into a `Vec<Shard>` and only then add them, so a row kept a thousand shards
-/// (megabytes) alive for the whole fight and the addition could not start
-/// until the last run had finished.  Here a lane builds ONE SHARD PER RUN,
-/// hands over a chunk of them, and goes straight back to fighting; the calling
-/// thread (the merger) adds each chunk the moment every run before it has
-/// arrived.  Peak shards drop from `count` to a couple of chunks per lane, and
-/// the additions overlap the fight instead of following it.
-///
-/// THE ADDITIONS THEMSELVES ARE UNTOUCHED: same values, same order, one run at
-/// a time.  A lane's shard is that run's own contribution, never a
-/// partly-summed one, because summing inside a lane would reassociate the
-/// additions and change the last bits of every answer.
-pub fn fold_parallel(
-    params: Arc<FightParams>,
-    acc: Shard,
-    from: u32,
-    count: u32,
-    seed: u64,
-    keep: bool,
-    threads: usize,
